\description{
A plugin extends a board as a whole, such as a frontend that draws the page,
the DAG or a document of \code{blockr.md}, and is core's one way to extend a board
besides block classes.
An app runs at most one plugin of each plugin class, and the app says which
plugins run, not the board: \code{\link[=serve]{serve()}} takes them in \code{plugins}, as rows that
give each plugin's class by its package and its id.
So a plugin is identified by the id of its class, and named by its class.
}
\details{
A plugin object holds the state that a plugin gives the board that an app
script builds, as a block holds its state, and \code{\link[=new_board]{new_board()}} takes such
objects in \code{plugins} for their state alone.
A package's constructor \verb{new_\{name\}_plugin()} returns one, made by
\code{new_plugin()}, of class \code{c("{name}_plugin", ..., "plugin")}.
The constructor's args are the plugin's state, such as the positions of the
DAG, which it passes to \code{new_plugin()} as a list by name, followed by the id
of the plugin's class, as \code{new_plugin(list(positions = positions), "dag_plugin")}.
The class vector, \code{class}, defaults to the id, and serves S3 dispatch alone.

The id of a plugin's class in full is \code{{pkg}-{id}}, as
\code{blockr.dag-dag_plugin}, where the package is the one that the constructor
lives in, since that package defines the class in JavaScript, in a module of
its own, whose default export the client registers under that id.
So an app script defines no plugin class, and \code{new_plugin()} raises an error
where the constructor that calls it lives outside a package.
A package lists each of its plugin classes in its \code{._BLOCKR_MODULES_}, by
its constructor, with its id, its name, the human-readable form of its id,
such as "Document" for \code{md_plugin}, which titles the plugin's panel, and its
module (see \code{\link[=read_blocks_yml]{read_blocks_yml()}}).
The function \code{available_plugins()} gives the plugin classes that the
available packages list, as a data frame with a row for each class, with
its package in \code{package} and its id in \code{id}, which identify the class.
The rest of a class, such as its name, stays with its package's listing.

Core defines two plugin classes beside \code{plugin}, since every page relies on
what each does: \code{frontend_plugin}, the class of a plugin that draws the page,
and \code{storage_plugin}, the class of a plugin through which every frontend
saves boards and opens them.
A package's class extends one of them or \code{plugin} itself, as
\code{c("session_plugin", "storage_plugin", "plugin")}.
Core's own plugins do so too, and none of them keeps any state.
Its basic frontend, which \code{new_basic_frontend_plugin()} makes, is of class
\code{c("basic_frontend_plugin", "frontend_plugin", "plugin")}.
Its storage plugin, which \code{new_file_storage_plugin()} makes, is of class
\code{c("file_storage_plugin", "storage_plugin", "plugin")}, and saves a board as
a download and opens an upload.
Its Edit board plugin, which \code{new_edit_board_plugin()} makes, is of class
\code{c("edit_board_plugin", "plugin")}.
It adds, renames and removes blocks, and adds, changes and removes links and
stacks.
Every frontend places it as it places any plugin with a UI.
An app runs it unless its rows leave it out, as it runs every plugin class
that \code{available_plugins()} gives.
So the user can add the first block to the board that \code{serve(new_board())}
draws.

The version of a plugin class is the version of the shape of its plugins'
state, a whole number from 1, which the class's method of \code{plugin_version()}
gives, and which is 1 for a class without one.
A package raises it as it changes that shape, so that the client migrates
the state of a plugin that a JSON board holds at an older version.
R calls the method on the plugin that the class's constructor makes, called
without args, and sends the version of the class of each of the app's
plugins in its reply to \code{hello}.
A plugin class that needs R gives its requests through its method of
\code{\link[=plugin_requests]{plugin_requests()}}, which R calls on such a plugin too.

The app's frontend may take arguments of \code{\link[=serve]{serve()}}, as the dock takes the
items of its bar in \code{navbar}, through its class's method of
\code{frontend_args()}.
As \code{serve()} is called, R calls the method on the plugin that the
frontend's constructor makes, with every argument in the \code{...} of
\code{serve()}.
The method takes the arguments that the frontend knows and refuses the
rest, naming the frontend.
It returns what the frontend reads of them, as a list by name.
R writes that list as a JSON object, and sends it in its reply to \code{hello},
so that no board or file sets it.
Core then calls the \code{render()} of the frontend's module with it, in \code{args}.
A method cannot hand the arguments that it does not know on to core's
method with \code{NextMethod()}, since R would hand on those that it took as
well, so each method refuses them itself.
Core's method for \code{frontend_plugin} takes no argument and returns an empty
list.
So core's basic frontend refuses every argument, and so does a frontend
whose class has no method of its own.

A plugin class may complete the state of its plugins on the board that a
script builds, through its method of \code{complete_plugin()}.
Once the board is valid, \code{\link[=new_board]{new_board()}} calls the method on each of the
board's plugins.
The method gets the board in \code{board}.
In \code{aliases}, it gets the names that the script gives the board's plugins,
each mapped to the ID of its plugin's class, as
\code{c(dag = "blockr.dag-dag_plugin")}.
It returns the plugin with its state completed, such as with each such name
turned into the ID of a class, and the board holds what it returns.
It may refuse a state that names what the board lacks, such as a block, so
that a script's mistake fails where the script builds its board.
Each method gets the board as \code{\link[=validate_board]{validate_board()}} passed it, before any
plugin completed its state, so the order of the plugins does not matter.
Core's method for \code{plugin} returns the plugin as it is.
The board holds each plugin under the ID of its class, so \code{new_board()}
refuses a result that is not a plugin of the same class.
R reads nothing of a JSON board but its format, so only \code{new_board()} calls
\code{complete_plugin()}, and the names exist only there.
}
